defmodule BeamPM.Billing do
  @moduledoc """
  MP6 (billing/usage dedup): `reconcile/4`, a hand-designed algorithm over the
  generated `BeamPM.Billing.UsageEvent` / `BeamPM.Billing.BillingReconciliation`
  structs above (records+fields SPARQL scoped to those two record types via
  `VALUES`, same shape as `beam4pm_types.ex.tmpl`).

  REQUIRED FIELD NAMES (a real constraint, not enforced by any gate):
  `reconcile/4` is hand-designed against SPECIFIC field names. A
  consumer's `usage_event` RecordType MUST declare exactly: `event_id`,
  `entitlement_id`, `metric_name`, `quantity`, `occurred_at`.
  `billing_reconciliation` MUST declare exactly: `entitlement_id`,
  `metric_name`, `total_quantity`, `applied_event_ids`, `period_start`,
  `period_end`. Renaming any of these still passes `gates/010_required.rq`
  and `gates/020_field_type_enum.rq`, but produces a real compile error at
  the struct field accesses -- found and disclosed by adversarial
  verification 2026-08-30, named explicitly rather than left as a silent
  trap.

  Mirrors the Erlang `beam4pm_billing:reconcile_billing/4` filter+fold
  exactly (same period filter, same dedup structure, same canonical
  summation order), following the `beam4pm_discovery.ex.tmpl` precedent of
  literal, hand-written algorithmic code rather than a per-field template.

  REAL BUG FOUND AND FIXED (2026-08-30) by adversarial verification: an
  earlier revision shipped `reconcile/1` (events) -> [reconciliations],
  deriving `period_start`/`period_end` AFTER THE FACT from whatever
  `occurred_at` values happened to be in ITS OWN input batch, with no
  external period argument at all -- silently dropping the exact
  anti-double-billing mechanism (an externally-supplied half-open
  `[period_start, period_end)` filter applied BEFORE dedup) this pack's own
  design doc mandated and its own falsifier had already verified.
  Mechanically reproduced: the same usage_event landing in both an
  "August" batch and a "September" batch (a realistic off-by-one query or
  at-least-once redelivery spanning a period rollover) was counted in BOTH
  periods' totals, since each period's own dedup set was internally
  duplicate-free and had no visibility into the other period's set.

  Fixed for real: `reconcile/4(events, entitlement_id, metric_name,
  {period_start, period_end})` filters to exactly one
  `{entitlement_id, metric_name}` partition AND
  `period_start <= occurred_at < period_end` (half-open, exclusive upper
  bound) BEFORE deduplicating and summing -- an event on or after
  `period_end` belongs to the NEXT period's call, never this one's, so the
  same `event_id` can never be double-counted across two adjacent calls.
  `period_start`/`period_end` on the returned struct are now the
  CALLER-SUPPLIED bounds, not derived via string min/max over
  `occurred_at` -- this also closes a secondary bug (period bounds could
  invert under mixed timestamp precision when derived from data;
  caller-supplied bounds have no such derivation step to invert).

  Dedup contract (unchanged from the prior revision, still correct on its
  own terms): within the filtered set, deduplicated by `event_id` via a
  map -- `Map.put_new/3` keeps the FIRST occurrence seen and ignores every
  later repeat, so neither the repeat count nor the arrival order of
  duplicates can change the surviving value or the total. `total_quantity`
  is summed by iterating `applied_event_ids` (sorted ascending), never the
  raw input list, so the total is a pure function of the deduplicated set.

  An empty filtered set returns a well-defined zero-total reconciliation
  for the given entitlement_id/metric_name/period, never a crash --
  "zero matching events" is a legitimate real outcome, not an error.
  """

  alias BeamPM.Billing.BillingReconciliation
  alias BeamPM.Billing.UsageEvent

  # REAL BUG FOUND AND FIXED (2026-08-30) by adversarial verification:
  # period_start >= period_end (an inverted or empty window -- a realistic
  # operator error: swapped call-site arguments) was never guarded. The
  # filter's own >= / < comparisons make an inverted window match ZERO
  # events unconditionally, so the function silently returned a
  # well-formed zero-total reconciliation with the nonsensical bounds
  # echoed back verbatim -- indistinguishable from a legitimate
  # "no usage this period" answer, silently under-billing a customer with
  # real usage in the (correctly-ordered) window the caller meant to ask
  # about. Reproduced against the real compiled module before this fix.
  # Now a typed refusal, never silent.
  @spec reconcile(
          [UsageEvent.t()],
          String.t(),
          String.t(),
          {String.t(), String.t()}
        ) :: BillingReconciliation.t() | {:error, {:invalid_period, String.t(), String.t()}}
  def reconcile(_events, _entitlement_id, _metric_name, {period_start, period_end})
      when period_start >= period_end do
    {:error, {:invalid_period, period_start, period_end}}
  end

  def reconcile(events, entitlement_id, metric_name, {period_start, period_end})
      when is_list(events) do
    in_scope =
      Enum.filter(events, fn %UsageEvent{} = event ->
        event.entitlement_id == entitlement_id and
          event.metric_name == metric_name and
          event.occurred_at >= period_start and
          event.occurred_at < period_end
      end)

    events_by_id =
      Enum.reduce(in_scope, %{}, fn %UsageEvent{} = event, acc ->
        Map.put_new(acc, event.event_id, event)
      end)

    applied_ids = events_by_id |> Map.keys() |> Enum.sort()

    total_quantity =
      Enum.reduce(applied_ids, 0.0, fn event_id, sum ->
        sum + Map.fetch!(events_by_id, event_id).quantity
      end)

    {:ok, reconciliation} =
      BillingReconciliation.new(%{
        entitlement_id: entitlement_id,
        metric_name: metric_name,
        total_quantity: total_quantity,
        applied_event_ids: applied_ids,
        period_start: period_start,
        period_end: period_end
      })

    reconciliation
  end
end
